\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A Wieferich prime (to base $2$) is a prime $p$ with $2^{p-1}\equiv1\pmod{p^2}$, meaning $p^2$ divides $2^{p-1}-1$. This forces $p$ to be odd.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Are there infinitely many such primes? \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics&oldid=1366636767#Number_theory}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Are there infinitely many primes satisfying the base-two Fermat congruence modulo their squares?

